::U6L1Q01::Substance use is {=a broad term that does not automatically mean misuse ~always proof of addiction ~always illegal ~the same as overdose}

::U6L1Q02::Substance misuse includes {=taking someone else's prescription ~taking one's medication exactly as directed ~seeking professional help ~using healthy coping}

::U6L1Q03::Tolerance means {=a reduced response after repeated exposure, not increased safety ~the substance is harmless ~addiction is diagnosed ~recovery is complete}

::U6L1Q04::Dependence may involve {=adaptation and withdrawal ~only peer pressure ~a single rumor ~automatic moral failure}

::U6L1Q05::A substance-use disorder involves {=a broader problematic pattern causing significant harm or impairment ~one isolated decision ~any prescribed use ~one risk factor}

::U6L1Q06::A risk factor {=may increase vulnerability without guaranteeing an outcome ~proves addiction ~cannot be changed ~is always genetic}

::U6L1Q07::Which is a protective factor? {=A supportive trusted adult ~Inaccurate perceived norms ~Continued peer pressure ~Limited coping}

::U6L1Q08::A perceived norm is {=a belief about what others commonly do that may be inaccurate ~a medical diagnosis ~a withdrawal symptom ~a prescription direction}

::U6L1Q09::Impairment can reduce {=judgment, reaction, coordination, and safe performance ~only legal knowledge ~the need for prevention ~all risk immediately}

::U6L1Q10::An overdose occurs when {=toxic effects exceed what the body can safely handle ~a person refuses a substance ~a prescription is stored securely ~a rumor spreads}

::U6L1Q11::Casey takes prescribed medicine exactly as directed. This is best classified as {=appropriate medication use ~prescription misuse ~proof of dependence ~proof of addiction}

::U6L1Q12::Casey gives prescribed medicine to a friend. This is {=prescription sharing and misuse ~appropriate treatment ~a protective factor ~recovery}

::U6L1Q13::A person reports needing more for a similar effect. The strongest conclusion is {=this matches tolerance and warrants concern, but does not alone diagnose addiction ~addiction is proven ~the substance is now safe ~treatment has failed}

::U6L1Q14::Stress, peer pressure, and limited coping are best interpreted as {=possible risk factors ~certain causes of addiction ~diagnoses ~protective factors}

::U6L1Q15::Clear goals, accurate information, and a trusted coach are {=protective factors ~withdrawal signs ~proof no risk exists ~forms of intoxication}

::U6L1Q16::“Everyone at school uses substances” should be evaluated as {=an unsupported perceived-norm claim ~a verified medical fact ~proof of safety ~a diagnosis}

::U6L1Q17::Repeated use is interfering with school and relationships. A peer should {=recognize concern and recommend qualified support without diagnosing ~declare a diagnosis ~use shame ~ignore the pattern}

::U6L1Q18::Which statement is non-stigmatizing? {=Addiction is a complex health condition influenced by multiple factors ~Addiction proves weak character ~People can stop instantly if they care ~Treatment is pointless}

::U6L1Q19::Return to use during recovery means {=continued professional support may be appropriate ~recovery is permanently impossible ~the person has bad character ~treatment can never help}

::U6L1Q20::Using a substance to escape stress may {=create additional health and safety concerns ~replace professional mental-health care ~guarantee healthy coping ~eliminate impairment}

::U6L1Q21::A fictional student faces pressure but wants to protect a scholarship. The goal functions as {=a protective factor supporting refusal ~a diagnosis ~a withdrawal sign ~proof risk is absent}

::U6L1Q22::Which reasoning correctly limits a conclusion? {=The evidence suggests concern, but qualified assessment is needed for diagnosis ~One sign proves addiction ~Risk factors guarantee the outcome ~A legal substance cannot cause harm}

::U6L1Q23::A medication is legal for one prescribed person. This means {=sharing it can still be misuse and unsafe ~it is safe for anyone ~directions are optional ~it cannot impair performance}

::U6L1Q24::Which response best supports prevention? {=Use accurate information, healthy coping, supportive relationships, and help-seeking ~Rely on “everyone does it” ~Hide concerns ~Treat risk as destiny}

::U6L1Q25::A student notices repeated risky behavior and worsening responsibilities. The best next step is {=seek support from a trusted adult or qualified professional ~diagnose the student publicly ~use shame ~wait until an emergency}

::U6L1Q26::Why can substance use affect future goals? {=Impairment and consequences may interfere with school, athletics, driving, or work ~Goals eliminate all effects ~Only illegal substances affect performance ~Consequences are always identical}

::U6L1Q27::Which evidence supports a multidimensional analysis? {=Missed practices, conflict, impaired judgment, and financial loss ~A single label with no facts ~A rumor about character ~An unsupported diagnosis}

::U6L1Q28::A student has family-history vulnerability and strong protective supports. The best conclusion is {=vulnerability may be increased, but genetics and risk do not determine the outcome ~addiction is certain ~protective factors guarantee no harm ~family disclosure is required}

::U6L1Q29::When withdrawal may be medically serious, a student should {=seek qualified medical help rather than attempt home detox ~give treatment instructions ~keep it secret ~assume it is harmless}

::U6L1Q30::Why is treating addiction as a health condition useful? {=It supports accurate assessment, treatment, recovery, and reduced stigma ~It excuses every unsafe choice ~It proves one cause ~It eliminates prevention}


